# User-declared Equatable instance for Point type — cross-dict dispatch.
#
# T-2109 added parent dispatch chaining: dict 3's = MethodDispatcher (no Point arm) now
# forwards to dict 2's = MethodDispatcher via a captured parent-scope reference. This
# fixes the cross-dict chaining gap (B-682).
#
# However, B-712 remains: the Point dispatch arm in dict 2's MethodDispatcher captures the
# Point binding (a type-constructor dict), not a TypeNode variant. The match evaluator's
# TypeNode path only handles builtin types; user-defined types fall through to
# primitive_eq(scrutinee, type_dict) which always returns false. The Point arm is dead code
# — the wildcard fallback fires instead. Both = calls still return Boolean.False.
# Tracked as B-712 (MethodDispatcher cannot match user-defined nominal types).
[Point: [type Point: [x: Int y: Int]]]
[instance Equatable
  [let a@Point]: [=: [fn [let p q]
    [and [= p.x q.x] [= p.y q.y]]]]]
[
  p1: [Point.Point x: 1 y: 2]
  p2: [Point.Point x: 1 y: 2]
  p3: [Point.Point x: 1 y: 3]
  # Both return Boolean.False — B-712: user-defined type dispatch arms are dead code.
  same: [= p1 p2]
  diff: [= p1 p3]
]
=== out
Dict({"p1": Variant(Point.Point, Dict({"x": Int(1), "y": Int(2)})), "p2": Variant(Point.Point, Dict({"x": Int(1), "y": Int(2)})), "p3": Variant(Point.Point, Dict({"x": Int(1), "y": Int(3)})), "same": Variant(Boolean.False, Null), "diff": Variant(Boolean.False, Null)})
